{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791432710,"nonce":"Ew+kTSu6Cg7Ey936jYCULH4oQ4k=","title":"Reflection returns inconsistent identities for strings, arrays, and type values","message":"The EnumReflection.hx regression imports haxe.macro.Context and Type.ValueType, then checks Type.typeof via Type.enumEq across primitives, instances, type literals, and enums. It also compares Type.getClass for a String, a typed Array, a Dynamic Array, and a null Array against their native Haxe results.\n\nThe runtime builds TClass(String) and TClass(Array) using primitive box ids 5 and 7, while compiler type literals use stable named class ids. Raw String and Array values also reach Type.getClass without the Dynamic header expected by that runtime helper. Type values stored in Dynamic can be scalar tokens or tagged boxes and must report TObject rather than being treated as instance pointers. A boxed null array must report a null class, not Array.\n\nNative Haxe passes EnumReflection.hx. The final v8 prototype also passes with pinned Cranelift, while the complete corpus and regression gates are pending. Earlier failed probes and metadata/name-resolution investigations are recorded in c4b7f062533d305ec1b59fb702987e7043584a73aabd7b031be2705b54df763c.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/rayzor-enum-reflection-native-v8.log, /tmp/rayzor-enum-reflection-pinned-v8.log, /tmp/rayzor-enum-reflection-pinned-v6.log.","files":null}]}